Social media followers feel like an audience, but you are renting them. The platform decides how many of them see a post, and that number has only ever gone one way. An email list is different: you hold it, and a message you send arrives.

For an independent shop that mostly means a few hundred people, not a few thousand. That is fine. A hundred local people who asked to hear from you is worth more than ten thousand who did not.

## When to ask

Timing does nearly all the work. Ask at the moment someone is most pleased with you, which is almost always **just after they have paid and liked what they got**.

Good moments:

- **While the card machine is thinking.** There is a dead five seconds there and it belongs to you.
- **When someone compliments something.** "Glad you liked it — want to know when we make it again?"
- **When you hand over a loyalty card**, digital or paper. They have already agreed to a relationship.
- **At the end of an appointment**, when a hairdresser or therapist is writing down the next one anyway.

Bad moments: on the way in, mid-queue, or when something has gone wrong.

## What to say

"Would you like to join our mailing list?" gives someone nothing to say yes to. Mailing lists are a thing people already have too many of.

Give a reason instead, and make it specific and small:

- "We email once a month with what's new. Want me to add you?"
- "We tell our list about new bakes the day before. Shall I put you on it?"
- "If you want first pick of the Christmas boxes, we email that list first."

Three things make those work: a **frequency** so they know what they are agreeing to, a **reason** that is worth something, and a **question they can decline** without awkwardness.

## How to collect it, practically

You do not need software to start.

| Method | Good for | Watch out for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A notebook by the till | Starting today, costs nothing | Handwriting; type it up weekly or it rots |
| A tablet or phone form | Accuracy, no transcribing | Someone has to hand it over and unlock it |
| A QR code to a short form | No hardware, works in the window | Needs a reason next to it, or it gets ignored |
| At loyalty sign-up | They are already giving you details | Must be a separate, optional choice |
| On the receipt | Read later, no pressure | Low response; good as a backup, not a main route |

Whichever you use, **ask for the first name as well**. It costs one extra field and makes everything you send afterwards sound like a person wrote it.

## The legal part, briefly

This is the bit most guides skip, and it is not complicated for a normal shop.

In the UK, marketing by email is covered by **PECR**, alongside UK GDPR for the data itself. For an individual — which includes a sole trader — the general rule is that **you need their consent before sending marketing email**.

There is a narrower route often called the **soft opt-in**: if someone bought something from you (or negotiated to), you gave them a clear chance to refuse marketing when you collected the address, and every message since has offered an easy way out, you may market **similar** products and services to them. It is useful, but it is narrower than people assume, and it does not cover an address someone gave you for a booking confirmation and nothing else.

What this means in practice:

- **A positive action is required.** An unticked box they tick, or saying yes out loud and you writing it down. Pre-ticked boxes and "by signing up you agree" do not count.
- **Keep a note of when and how** each person agreed. A date column in your spreadsheet is enough for a small shop.
- **Every marketing email needs an unsubscribe** that works, and acting on it promptly.
- **Say who you are** in the message. If you are a limited company, the Companies Act also wants your registered name, number and office in business emails — usually a line in the footer.
- **Do not buy a list.** It has no consent behind it, and it will do more damage to your sending reputation than the list is worth.

That is a summary rather than legal advice. The ICO publishes plain-English guidance on direct marketing and it is worth half an hour of your own reading before the first send.

